{"summary": "Episode covering two main topics: music practice sound management and freelance business practices.

MUSIC PRACTICE: User concerned about neighbors hearing their music practice. Assistant suggested: thick rugs/carpet padding under practice area to block downward sound transmission; soundproofing panels (affordable on Amazon); electronic drum kits with mesh heads and low-volume cymbals. Warning that late-night practice may disturb neighbors more than daytime because sound carries strangely when streets are quiet. Additional tips: pad instrument stands, use practice mutes. For guitar: soundhole covers have minimal effect; recommended clip-on piezoelectric pickups (LR Baggs, DiMarzio brands) with small headphone amps for silent practice. Advice on setting boundaries with family: have open conversation, establish clear practice schedule, use simple signal like sticky note on door.

FREELANCE BUSINESS: User (6 months freelance designer) seeking client management advice. Assistant recommendations: 1) Always use contracts specifying scope, timeline, and payment terms upfront; 2) Include revision limits (2-3 rounds) to prevent scope creep; 3) Require 50% deposit before starting, 50% upon completion to protect against clients vanishing mid-project - this is standard practice and pushback can be a red flag; 4) Offer 10% discount for payment within 10 days to incentivize fast payment. For handling vague project briefs to avoid underpricing: ask clarifying questions, offer price ranges instead of fixed prices, use hourly rates for open-ended work, or propose discovery calls to clarify details before committing."}

